## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of handling duplicate-heavy inputs efficiently and the ability to implement in-place sorting algorithms with optimized space usage.

## Key Concepts
- **3-way partitioning**: Separates elements into three groups: less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot
- **In-place quicksort**: Achieves O(1) auxiliary space (excluding recursion stack)
- **Pivot selection**: Random sampling avoids worst-case behavior on sorted inputs; median-of-three provides more robust estimates
- **Complexity analysis**: Best/average O(n log n); worst O(n²) when pivot consistently produces imbalanced partitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++; i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++;
    }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Best case**: O(n log n) — partitions remain balanced
- **Average case**: O(n log n) — expected with random pivot
- **Worst case**: O(n²) — occurs with sorted input and poor pivot (e.g., always picking first or last element)

## Pivot Selection Trade-offs
| Strategy | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| Random | Simple, avoids adversarial input | Still possible to pick poor pivots |
| Median-of-three | More robust estimates | Slightly more computation |

The 3-way partition is particularly valuable for arrays with many duplicates, as it reduces unnecessary swaps and comparisons for elements equal to the pivot.

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design algorithms with provable performance guarantees, handle pathological inputs gracefully, and understand trade-offs between different sorting approaches. It's particularly relevant for large-scale systems where worst-case behavior impacts reliability.

## Key Concepts
- **Introsort**: Hybrid algorithm combining quicksort's average-case efficiency with heapsort's worst-case guarantees
- **3-way partitioning**: Dutch national flag algorithm that efficiently handles duplicate keys
- **Depth monitoring**: Switches to heapsort when recursion depth exceeds 2⌊log₂(n)⌋ to maintain O(n log n) worst-case
- **Space complexity**: O(log n) stack space through tail-call optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n || 1));

  function sort(left, right, depth) {
    if (left >= right) return;

    if (depth === 0) {
      heapSort(arr, left, right);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = threeWayPartition(arr, left, right);

    // Sort smaller partition first (tail recursion optimization)
    if (lt - left < right - gt) {
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
    } else {
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
    }
  }

  sort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a practical data structure for real-world features (auto-complete) and how to extend it with per-word frequencies.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequency tracking
- DFS/BFS to collect candidate words from a prefix
- Sorting by frequency then lexicographic order
- Edge cases: missing prefix, zero matches, tie handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.freq = 0; // frequency for word ending here
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.freq += 1;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (n, path) => {
      if (n.freq > 0) results.push([path, n.freq]);
      for (const [ch, child] of n.next) dfs(child, path + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, prefix);
    results.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return results.slice(0, K).map(v => v[0]);
  }
}
```

